      "source_regions": [
        "page-5-r0004"
      ],
      "text": "Mental model: traffic jam!",
      "type": "heading"
    },
    {
      "complexity": "complex",
      "detailed_figure_description": "A closed circuit is drawn as a traffic jam of small car-like boxes labeled “e⁻.” The boxes form a loop. A zigzag resistor labeled R interrupts the top of the loop, while a battery labeled V interrupts the bottom. The battery’s positive terminal is on the left and its negative terminal is on the right. The crowded electron boxes illustrate the traffic-jam mental model for current through a resistor.",
      "figure_alternative": "Traffic-jam model of electrons crowded around a closed circuit containing a resistor R and a battery V.",
      "id": "page-5-s0005",
      "page": 5,
      "source_regions": [
        "page-5-r0005"
      ],
      "type": "figure"
    },
    {
      "id": "page-5-s0006",
      "level": 2,
      "page": 5,
      "source_regions": [
        "page-5-r0007"
      ],
      "text": "Example",
      "type": "heading"
    },
    {
      "id": "page-5-s0007",
      "page": 5,
      "source_regions": [
        "page-5-r0006",
        "page-5-r0008",
        "page-5-r0009",
        "page-5-r0011"
      ],
      "text": "A flashlight bulb draws 300mA from its 1.5 V battery. What’s the resistance of the bulb? If the battery degrades and only produces 1.2 V how would the current change?",
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "complexity": "simple",
      "detailed_figure_description": null,
      "figure_alternative": "Sketch of a glowing flashlight with a bulb and two batteries inside.",
      "id": "page-5-s0008",
      "page": 5,
      "source_regions": [
        "page-5-r0010"
      ],
      "type": "figure"
    },
    {
      "id": "page-5-s0009",
      "normalized_math": "I = \\frac{V}{R} \\Rightarrow R = \\frac{V}{I} = \\frac{1.5\\,\\mathrm{V}}{0.30\\,\\mathrm{A}} = 5.0\\,\\Omega",
      "page": 5,
      "source_regions": [
        "page-5-r0012"
      ],
      "spoken_math_alternative": "Current equals voltage divided by resistance. Therefore, resistance equals voltage divided by current, which equals 1.5 volts divided by 0.30 amperes, or 5.0 ohms.",
      "type": "formula"
    },
    {
      "id": "page-5-s0010",
      "normalized_math": "V \\to 1.2\\,\\mathrm{V}:\\quad I = \\frac{1.2\\,\\mathrm{V}}{5.0\\,\\Omega} = 0.24\\,\\mathrm{A} = 240\\,\\mathrm{mA}",
      "page": 5,
      "source_regions": [
        "page-5-r0013"
      ],
      "spoken_math_alternative": "When the voltage decreases to 1.2 volts, current equals 1.2 volts divided by 5.0 ohms, which is 0.24 amperes, or 240 milliamperes.",
      "type": "formula"
    }
  ],
